      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the certificate.\n\nThis is part 2 of 4 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 14, project number 4009997, NMi (Netherlands), pages 5-7 of 13.\n\nContent:\n1. Software versions table (continued from page 4, part 1): Software part code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality) with versions 03.00.03, 03.00.04, 03.02.00, 03.03.02, 03.03.03, then on page 5: 03.03.06, 03.04.00, 03.04.02.\n2. Software part code 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater): 02.00.02, 02.00.04\n3. Software part code 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater): 02.00.01, 02.00.05\n4. Table 4: Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant[1] - Measurement MCU versions, Communication MCU (4G), Communication MCU (NB-IoT)\n5. Footnote [1] about software version display.\n6. Table 5: General characteristics - Reduced bore - AC mains powered. Meter sizes DN40-DN600, accuracy classes 1 and 2, flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, ratio Q3/Q1.\n7. Table 6: General characteristics - Reduced bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered. Same structure.\n\nDocument-level info:\n- certificate number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 14\n- scheme: A (from \"2013-A-NL1\")\n- project_number: 4009997\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: The Netherlands\n- oiml_issuer_id: NMi — probably just \"NMi\" visible; the issuing authority section: NMi. Since this is a fragment, maybe include issuing_authority partially? The instructions say extract for content present in this fragment only. NMi and Netherlands appear. But issuing authority name/address not fully present. I could include member_state in certificate.\n\nThe certified type: AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant (water meter, R49). Category: \"Water meter\" — since R49 is water meters. Type designation: FEW45Y.Z. Also model_family with models — actually the cert covers a family of instruments with meter sizes DN40-DN600 in two power variants (AC mains powered; Battery/Renewable energy powered).\n\nMatrix tables:\n- Software versions table(s): can be represented as matrix_tables with name \"Software versions — 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality)\", columns: software_version, crc_checksum, remarks; rows.\n- Table 4: Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant with sections Measurement, 4G, NB-IoT. Rows include MCU type.\n- Table 5: Reduced bore - AC mains powered.\n- Table 6: Reduced bore - Battery/Renewable energy powered.\n\nRecommendation: R49, edition 2013 (from \"OIML R49-1:2013\" referenced; the cert number \"R49/2013\" implies edition 2013). accuracy_classes: [1, 2]. Scheme: A.\n\nModel family: The cert covers a family of instruments. Models could be identified by meter size × power supply × accuracy class. The models vary by DN size, accuracy class, and power supply. That's a lot of combinations. Per the schema, model_level characteristics would be attribute × model matrix. With tables 5 and 6, the axis could be \"meter size\" and config axis \"power supply\" and \"accuracy class\".\n\nI think the best representation:\n- model_family.family_name: \"AquaMaster4\" (family of instruments). Actually the family name — \"AquaMaster4\" is mentioned in Table 4 as \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\". The general characteristics says \"family of instruments\". So family_name: \"AquaMaster4\".\n- models: one per meter size? Or per size × power? Hmm. The config_level axis is \"power supply\" with conditions \"AC mains powered\" and \"Battery / Renewable energy powered\". Accuracy class is also a varying dimension.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll represent:\n- matrix_tables: two entries for Table 5 and Table 6, each with columns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_min, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1; column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]. Rows as objects.\n- model_family.models: one per meter size: DN40...DN600. That's 13 sizes: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 — that's 15 sizes. Let me count Table 5: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 = 15 sizes.\n- characteristics.model_level: q3 permanent flow per model? But values differ by accuracy class and power supply. Hmm.\n\nPerhaps better: config_level entries:\n- attribute \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", axis \"power supply\", values: [{condition: \"AC mains powered\", value: ...}, ...] — but value varies by model too. This is a 3-D matrix (model × power × class), which doesn't fit cleanly. The matrix_tables preserve the full data; model_level can carry the per-model Q3 (which is the same across classes within a power config: e.g. DN40 Q3=25 in both classes; AC: DN40 25; battery: DN40 25). Actually Q3 is same for both classes within a power config for all sizes: AC: 25, 40, 63, 100, 160, 160, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300. Battery: same Q3 values: 25, 40, 63, 100, 160, 160, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300. Yes, Q3 identical across power configs too! So Q3 per model (meter size) is well-defined.\n\nQ4 = 1.25 × Q3, also same across configs: 31.25, 50, 78.75, 125, 200, 200, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875. Same in both tables. So model_level: q3 and q4 per model.\n\nQ1 and Q2 vary by class and power config — keep in matrix_tables and/or config_level. I'll put them in matrix_tables fully and maybe config_level with axis \"power_supply / accuracy_class\". To keep JSON manageable, I'll rely on matrix_tables for Q1/Q2 and add model_level for q3, q4.\n\nAlso note in the fragment: \"Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and the ratio Q2/Q1 = 1,6.\" etc. — these are notes; I could capture as description or footnotes? They're table notes, not footnotes with markers. I can put in matrix_tables.description.\n\nThe software tables: matrix_tables entries. Let me structure:\n\nmatrix_tables:\n1. name: \"Software versions — part code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality)\"\n   columns: [\"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"]\n   rows: [\n     {software_version: \"03.00.03\", crc_checksum: \"B26AFE6B\", remarks: null},\n     {software_version: \"03.00.04\", crc_checksum: \"6E98963A\", remarks: null},\n     {software_version: \"03.02.00\", crc_checksum: \"E12E5C04\", remarks: null},\n     {software_version: \"03.03.02\", crc_checksum: \"D4D1E40B\", remarks: \"HMI display bug fixes. Low flow output bug fix.\"},\n     {software_version: \"03.03.03\", crc_checksum: \"CEEF45F1\", remarks: \"Modbus communication bug fix.\"},\n     {software_version: \"03.03.06\", crc_checksum: \"DAC51328\", remarks: null},\n     {software_version: \"03.04.00\", crc_checksum: \"277BA336\", remarks: null},\n     {software_version: \"03.04.02\", crc_checksum: \"66624063\", remarks: null}\n   ]\n\n2. name: \"Software versions — part code 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater)\"\n   rows: [{02.00.02, 84EBD1C8}, {02.00.04, 5D2D2DDD}]\n\n3. name: \"Software versions — part code 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater)\"\n   rows: [{02.00.01, C28EAA16}, {02.00.05, F517559F}]\n\n4. Table 4: \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\"\n   columns: [\"mcu\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\"]\n   rows: \n   - Measurement MCU: 01.02.00/94E6FAE9, 01.03.00/A6C913AF, 01.03.01/2BB4AE79, 01.03.03/2358C8FF\n   - 4G Communication MCU: 01.02.00/AE3D9862, 01.03.00/3B33D2C8, 01.03.01/FDE7CBB6, 01.03.02/CD0C1881, 01.05.01/947F95FD\n   - NB-IoT Communication MCU: 01.03.00/E5742163, 01.03.01/EC4FF9C6, 01.03.02/C8CABC2D, 01.03.03/31459473\n\n5. Table 5: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments — Reduced bore, AC mains powered\"\n   columns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1_minimum\", \"q2_transitional\", \"q3_permanent\", \"q4_overload\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"]\n   column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]\n   rows: 25 rows (each size × class combination).\n\nLet me transcribe Table 5 rows:\n- DN40, 1, 0.05, 0.08, 25, 31.25, 500\n- DN40, 2, 0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000\n- DN50, 1, 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, 2, 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n- DN65, \"1 & 2\", 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n- DN80, \"1 & 2\", 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n- DN100, 1, 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, 2, 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN125, 1, 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN125, 2, 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN150, \"1 & 2\", 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n- DN200, \"1 & 2\", 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n- DN250, 1, 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, 2, 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n- DN300, 1, 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, 2, 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN350, 1, 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN350, 2, 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN400, \"1 & 2\", 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN450, \"1 & 2\", 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN500, 1, 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500\n- DN500, 2, 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000\n- DN600, 1, 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500\n- DN600, 2, 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000\n\nNote DN600 class 1 Q1 = 12.6 (interesting, 12.6 not 12.8 — that's what's written; Q3/Q1 = 6300/12.6 = 500. Yes correct.)\n\nTable 6 rows:\n- DN40, \"1 & 2\", 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n- DN50, \"1 & 2\", 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n- DN65, 1, 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\n- DN65, 2, 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n- DN80, 1, 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n- DN80, 2, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- DN100, 1, 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN100, 2, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN125, 1, 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN125, 2, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN150, \"1 & 2\", 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n- DN200, \"1 & 2\", 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n- DN250, 1, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n- DN250, 2, 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n- DN300, 1, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN300, 2, 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n- DN350, 1, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN350, 2, 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n- DN400, 1, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN400, 2, 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n- DN450, 1, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN450, 2, 12.5, 20, 2500, 3125, 200\n- DN500, 1, 100, 160, 4000, 5000, 40\n- DN500, 2, 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100\n- DN600, 1, 157.5, 252, 6300, 7875, 40\n- DN600, 2, 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\nNote: There's a typo in the HTML `<td>40</td>` for DN600 class1 ratio — it says `<td>40</td>` (malformed \"7875</td><td>40</td>\"). The ratio should be 40 (6300/157.5 = 40). Yes 40 is correct.\n\nDecimal conversions: 0,05 → 0.05, etc. All done above.\n\nFootnotes:\n- marker \"[1]\" or \"1\": text: \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — also revision 14. The schema has number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued. Revision — there's revision_history in schema. I can add revision \"14\" to revision_history? Revision history requires date and changes; not present. Maybe include certificate.revision? Schema doesn't have it, but \"Don't fabricate\" — I could omit, or include revision number in number string. Hmm. The number field could be \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and I might add a \"revision\": \"14\" — the schema doesn't define it but extra fields... Better to stay close to schema. I'll add revision_history entry with revision \"14\" and omit date/changes? The schema says revision (string), date, changes. Without date/changes, maybe include {\"revision\": \"14\"} only. That seems reasonable and non-fabricating. Alternatively put in number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 14\". I'll include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"14\"}] — hmm, could be odd. Actually I'll include \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and \"revision\": \"14\" as extra field on certificate. Extra fields are generally acceptable in JSON extraction as long as parseable. I'll add it.\n\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"4009997\"\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not given explicitly (NMi is issuer name). Omit.\n\nissuing_authority: name: \"NMi\" — the fragment shows NMi as header. Address not present. person_responsible not present. I'll include name only. Actually the instructions say omit sections not present — NMi is present, so include name only.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (R49 is water meters; AquaMaster4 is a water meter — Sensus). \n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"] — the fragment mentions \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\". Include.\n- description: maybe \"Family of water meters, reduced bore, AC mains powered or battery/renewable energy powered.\" Something derived. Could include.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: one per meter size (15 sizes). model_id: \"DN40\" ... \"DN600\". Attributes? Could note both power variants exist per size. Keep models as size IDs.\n\ncharacteristics:\n- type_level: maybe \"accuracy_classes\": {value: [1,2]}? Hmm, structured value... Accuracy classes 1 and 2. Could put accuracy_classes in recommendation instead. recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2]. Good.\n- model_level: \n  - permanent_flow_rate_q3 per model: values 25...6300 m³/h\n  - overload_flow_rate_q4 per model\n  - Also could include q1/q2 but they vary by class & power; skip (in matrix tables).\n- config_level:\n  - attribute \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", axis \"power_supply\", values: [{condition: \"AC mains powered, accuracy class 1\", value: ...}] — too complex. I'll skip config_level for q1/q2 since matrix tables capture it. Or add axis \"power supply and accuracy class\" with conditions like \"AC mains powered / class 1\". That's 4 combos × values vary per model — can't. Skip.\n\nmatrix_tables descriptions: include the note text about freely chosen flow rates. Include as description for tables 5 and 6.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\"\n- edition: 2013\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2]\n\nfootnotes:\n- marker \"1\" or \"[1]\": text as above.\n\nAlso maybe components? The software part codes could be components: \"Software part code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality)\" etc. These are software components of the meter. The schema components[]: role, type_designations, alternatives, characteristics. I could record three components:\n- role: \"software (full functionality)\", type_designations: [\"3KXF208402U0113\"]\n- role: \"software (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater)\", type_designations: [\"3KXF208402U0313\"]\n- role: \"software (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater)\", type_designations: [\"3KXF208402U0513\"]\nWith characteristics? Could embed software versions but matrix tables handle that. I'll include components with roles and type_designations, characteristics empty or omitted.\n\nHmm, alternatives: the three software part codes are alternatives? \"Full functionality\" vs sensor updater variants — they're different firmware builds, likely alternatives depending on configuration. I'd say alternatives: \"OR\" for the three. Actually U0113 full functionality vs U0313 and U0513 — the latter two are subsets (\"updater\"). They might be alternatives. I'll set alternatives: \"OR\" on the three entries? Each component entry has its own alternatives field. Setting \"OR\" on all three implies they're alternatives to each other. I'll do that.\n\nLet me also capture \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\" footnote marker [1] relates to software versions displayed.\n\nOutput JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"revision\": \"14\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"4009997\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"],\n    \"description\": \"Family of water meters (reduced bore), AC mains powered or battery / renewable energy powered, accuracy classes 1 and 2, meter sizes DN40 to DN600.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"AquaMaster4\",\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN50\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN65\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN80\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN100\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN125\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN150\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN200\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN250\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN300\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN350\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN400\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN450\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN500\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN600\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [...],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"overload_flow_rate_q4\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [ ... ],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]\n  },\n  \"footnotes\": [\n    {\"marker\": \"1\", \"text\": \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nFor model_level values with structured form: rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be object {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. In the R137 example, model_level values are given as {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} without unit_symbol objects. So the unit lives at the attribute level (\"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"). I'll follow the example format: {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...}.\n\nQ3 per model: DN40: 25, DN50: 40, DN65: 63, DN80: 100, DN100: 160, DN125: 160, DN150: 400, DN200: 630, DN250: 1000, DN300: 1600, DN350: 1600, DN400: 2500, DN450: 2500, DN500: 4000, DN600: 6300.\n\nQ4: 31.25, 50, 78.75, 125, 200, 200, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875.\n\nMatrix table column names: use snake_case: meter_size, accuracy_class, minimum_flow_rate_q1, transitional_flow_rate_q2, permanent_flow_rate_q3, overload_flow_rate_q4, ratio_q3_q1.\n\naccuracy_class values: \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\" — keep as strings since some are combined. Or numbers where single. Mixed types in a column is awkward; use strings \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\"? Or \"1 and 2\". I'll keep \"1 & 2\" as in source. Actually to be safe, use strings for all: \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\". Hmm, rule says numbers not strings for values... but these are class labels, not measurements. I'll use strings for the combined and numbers for single? Inconsistent. I'll use strings throughout for accuracy_class to keep column type consistent. Actually the R137 example used strings for size. Accuracy class is a classifier too. Strings fine.\n\nRemarks \"-\" → null.\n\nTable 4 columns: communication_interface (or mcu_section), mcu, software_version, crc_checksum. The rowspan remark: \"Software identification for Measurement MCU\" / \"Software identification for Communication MCU\". Sections: Measurement, 4G, NB-IoT. So rows: {section: \"Measurement\", mcu: \"Measurement MCU\", version, crc}. Simpler: columns [\"interface\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\"], where interface ∈ {\"Measurement\", \"4G\", \"NB-IoT\"}. I'll add mcu_role column too: \"Measurement MCU\" / \"Communication MCU\".\n\nSoftware tables 1-3: part_code column? Each table separate, name includes part code. Also add columns software_version, crc_checksum, remarks.\n\nDescriptions for tables 5/6: \"Flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3 and Q4 can be freely chosen as long as: Values Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013. Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and the ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6. Values mentioned for Q3 and Q4 are maximum values and the ratio Q4/Q3 = 1.25. The ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40.\" Convert commas: \"1,6\" → 1.6, \"1,25\" → 1.25.\n\nComponents: three software components. role: \"measurement software\" etc. Let me write:\n- {\"role\": \"software (full functionality)\", \"type_designations\": [\"3KXF208402U0113\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\"}\n- {\"role\": \"software (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater)\", \"type_designations\": [\"3KXF208402U0313\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\"}\n- {\"role\": \"software (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater)\", \"type_designations\": [\"3KXF208402U0513\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\"}\n\nMaybe also add firmware MCU components from Table 4? Not necessary.\n\nNow assemble JSON. Keep it valid: watch the \"1 & 2\" ampersand — fine in JSON.\n\nLet me write out fully.",
